Looking at the type declarations and notes, the key challenge is that the geospatial camera clamps pitch based on radius (note 7, 45): at the starting radius of 4×planetRadius, pitch is forced to 0.001. Note 37 tells us that setting `limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null` allows full pitch at any radius, and that a limits change takes effect at the next yaw/pitch/radius write that changes the value.

So the solution is: disable the radius-based pitch clamp, then write the pitch. Since the write changes the value (from 0.001 to our target), it triggers the updated limits to apply.

FILE: app/src/globe-tilt.ts
```ts
import type { GeospatialCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function tiltGlobeCamera(camera: GeospatialCamera, pitch: number): void {
    camera.limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale = null;
    camera.pitch = pitch;
}
```

I set `limits.pitchDisabledRadiusScale` to `null` to remove the radius-based pitch clamp (which would otherwise force pitch to ~0 at the fully-zoomed-out starting radius and re-clamp it on every future zoom), then wrote the desired pitch — that write both triggers the updated limits to take effect and sets the camera's view angle. I'm not sure whether `setGeospatialOrientation` with only a `pitch` field would preserve the other values or reset them, so I used a direct property write which the notes confirm is a valid "write" that applies clamping.
